  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2024 by Peter Lang Verlag within the established \"Travail et Société / Work and Society\" series, this monograph by Silvia Carbone examines how knowledge and practical knowledge shape the working practices of social workers. The work is currently in its early circulation phase, with limited citation data available. Library holdings indicate modest uptake in institutional collections.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Silvia Carbone's <em>Knowledge and practical knowledge</em> addresses a core question in the sociology of professional practice: how do social workers integrate theoretical knowledge with situated, experiential knowing to inform their professional judgment and action? The book situates itself within established scholarly discourse on knowledge management, organizational learning, and the epistemic dimensions of skilled work. Its conceptual framework draws on knowledge value chains, the role of affect in professional understanding, and the relational dimensions of knowledge work—moving beyond a simple knowledge-versus-practice binary to explore how these dimensions are \"woven together\" in the actual conduct of social work.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The monograph is positioned within a rigorous, peer-reviewed publication context. It appears in volume 88 of Peter Lang's \"Travail et Société\" series, a long-running venue focused on labour, employment, and work-society relations. Companion volumes in the series address labour history, union organizing, workplace diversity, and social policy implementation—indicating that Carbone's inquiry into professional knowledge practices occupies a recognized niche within European labour and organizational studies.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation and early reception metrics remain sparse at present. No citations are currently recorded in OpenAlex, which is typical for a work published in 2024 and reflects the lag between publication and scholarly referencing rather than an absence of relevance. The work has been registered in OpenAIRE and is discoverable through standard library systems, with three Open Library holdings recorded. These modest indicators are consistent with specialized academic monographs from university presses, which typically achieve their scholarly audience gradually through professional networks and targeted adoption in graduate seminars and research projects.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The substantive focus on how social workers synthesize knowledge and action—bridging organizational structures, professional relationships, and individual judgment—addresses persistent questions in occupational sociology and professional practice studies. The work's integration of concepts spanning knowledge management, organizational sociology, and practice theory suggests it may be of interest to scholars of professional expertise, workplace learning, and the sociology of social work specifically.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
